{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 10–15 days show a persistent rise (higher highs / higher lows), the model level is systematically too low. Apply an additive uplift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger by weeks 3–4).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors increase with lead time. Use a ramp correction (e.g., +X by day 5, +2X by day 15, +3X by day 30) rather than a flat shift.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast follows the direction but with delayed magnitude (especially around breakouts). If recent slope is positive and increasing, increase forecast slope (steepen the path), not just the intercept.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after strength: after prices push to new local highs, the model tends to fade back toward ~68–70, while realized prices keep trending higher. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is above its 20–30d average and the average is rising.",
    "Volatility compression: realized moves are larger than forecasted moves during this regime. Inflate forecast volatility (wider swings) conditional on rising trend/volatility in the recent history.",
    "Late-horizon catch-up but still low: in some cases the model starts lifting only near the end (days ~25–30) yet remains below realized. Start the uplift earlier (from ~day 5–10) when trend conditions are met."
  ]
}
